<h2>Fence styles we build</h2>

<p><strong>Privacy fences.</strong> Most of our fence work is solid board privacy fencing in pressure-treated lumber: vertical boards on posts and horizontal backer rails, typically 6 feet high where the local by-law allows. In Carleton Place we fenced in a backyard so the homeowners have a private space around their gazebo where the yard used to open onto a shared lawn.</p>

<p><strong>Lattice tops and privacy screens.</strong> A lattice top softens a tall fence and lets some light through. A shorter screen section can hide a patio, a hot tub or the neighbour's window without fencing the whole yard. In Ottawa's Alta Vista area we built a pressure-treated lattice fence for exactly that kind of privacy.</p>

<p><strong>Shared and multi-yard fences.</strong> Neighbours often split a new fence along a shared line. In one Carleton Place job we built 6 foot privacy fences for three neighbouring homes at once, so the whole row matches.</p>

<p><strong>Gates.</strong> Single walk gates, wide double gates for a trailer or mower, and gates framed into a lattice top. On a Carleton Place side yard we built a single lattice-top gate between the house and the existing fence, and a wide double gate with a header beam across the top on the other side.</p>

<h2>Before the first post hole</h2>

<p><strong>Property lines.</strong> Know where your property line is before the fence is laid out. If there's any doubt, a survey and a conversation with your neighbour now can save a lot of trouble later.</p>

<p><strong>Utility locates.</strong> Call before you dig: utility locates are required before any digging, and post holes go deep enough to reach buried lines. In Ontario, locates are requested through Ontario One Call.</p>

<p><strong>By-laws.</strong> Fence heights, corner-lot rules and pool enclosure requirements are set by each municipality, whether that's the City of Ottawa, the Town of Carleton Place, Beckwith, Mississippi Mills or Perth. It's worth checking your local by-law before we settle on a design. We don't quote heights here because they vary from town to town.</p>

<h2>Posts that stand up to frost</h2>

<p>Ottawa Valley winters heave anything that isn't set properly, so the posts are the most important part of a fence. They're set on solid footings designed to resist frost heave, with exterior-grade hardware throughout. We'll talk about post spacing and layout for your yard at the estimate.</p>

<h2>Gates that don't sag</h2>

<p>A gate is the part of a fence that gets used every day, and it's usually the first thing to fail. We build gate frames square and braced, and hang them on hardware sized for the gate's weight and width, from a single walk gate to a wide double gate.</p>

<h2>Staining and maintenance</h2>

<p>Pressure-treated wood needs time to dry before it takes a stain well, and how long depends on the lumber and the weather. After that, a stain or sealer every few years keeps it looking good and slows weathering. Ask us when your fence is done and we'll tell you what to look for before staining.</p>

<h2>How a fence job works</h2>

<p>You send photos or a rough sketch of where the fence should go. We visit to measure, check the grade and gate locations, and talk through height and style. You get a written estimate in Canadian dollars, labour-only or with materials, before any work starts. Then we set the posts, build the panels and gates, and clean up so the yard is left tidy. You'll get a schedule with your quote. Workmanship is covered by our one-year limited <a href="{{ url('/warranty') }}">warranty</a>.</p>

<h2>Why hire Zanini Renovations for your fence</h2>

<p>You deal directly with the owner, Jonas Zanini, from the estimate to the final walkthrough. We're fully insured, and our certificate of insurance is available before the job starts. We also take on fencing for commercial properties and multi-unit sites, and most of the carpentry and renovation work we do for homeowners, we also do in condos and commercial spaces.</p>

